{"name":"Midnight Pearl","hex":"#5f6c74","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"midnight","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The middle of the night: the sixth temporal hour, equidistant between sunset and sunrise.","Twelve o'clock at night exactly."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Utterly dark or black."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/midnight"},{"term":"pearl","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A shelly concretion, usually rounded, and having a brilliant luster, with varying tints, found in the mantle, or between the mantle and shell, of certain bivalve mollusks, especially in the pearl oysters and river mussels, and sometimes in certain univalves. It is usually due to a secretion of shelly substance around some irritating foreign particle. Its substance is the same as nacre, or mother-of-pearl. Round lustrous pearls are used in jewellery.\nNacre; mother-of-pearl.\n A white color inspired by the shade of pearls.","Nacre; mother-of-pearl."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To set or adorn with pearls, or with mother-of-pearl.","To cause to resemble pearls in shape; to make into small round grains."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pearl"}],"entries":[{"title":"Midnight","blocks":[{"text":"Midnight is the transition time from one day to the next – the moment when the date changes, on the local official clock time for any particular jurisdiction. By clock time, midnight is the opposite of noon, differing from it by 12 hours."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Tour_de_l%27horloge_de_la_gare_de_Metz_%C3%A0_Minuit_%28juin_2019%29_%28cropped%29.JPG/330px-Tour_de_l%27horloge_de_la_gare_de_Metz_%C3%A0_Minuit_%28juin_2019%29_%28cropped%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Midnight","wikidata":"Q36402"},{"title":"Pearl","blocks":[{"text":"A pearl is a hard, shiny object produced within the soft tissue of a living shelled mollusk. Like the shell of a mollusk, a pearl is composed of calcium carbonate in a crystalline form, which is deposited in concentric layers. More commercially valuable pearls are perfectly round and smooth, but many other shapes, known as baroque pearls, can occur. The highest quality of natural pearls have been highly valued as gemstones and objects of beauty for many centuries. In literature, pearls have become a metaphor for something rare, fine, admirable, and valuable."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Various_pearls.jpg/330px-Various_pearls.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pearl","wikidata":"Q43436"},{"title":"Midnight blue","blocks":[{"text":"Midnight blue is a dark shade of blue named for its resemblance to the apparently blue color of a moonlit night sky around a full moon. Midnight blue is identifiably blue to the eye in sunlight or full-spectrum light, but can appear black under certain more limited spectra sometimes found in artificial lighting. It is similar to navy, which is also a dark blue."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/D%C3%BCsseldorf_Midnight_view.jpg/330px-D%C3%BCsseldorf_Midnight_view.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Midnight_blue","wikidata":"Q947638"},{"title":"Pearl (color)","blocks":[{"text":"The color pearl is a pale tint of off-white. It is a representation of the average color of a pearl."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c3/PerlmuttAusst.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pearl_(color)","wikidata":"Q7158021"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Midnight_Mist.jpg/500px-Midnight_Mist.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=p